.TH WAYHUD 1 "October 2026" "wayhud 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wayhud \- universal modern suckless Wayland on-screen HUD (GTK CSS styled)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wayhud
[\fB\-s\fR \fIstyle\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR \fIname\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wayhud
is a minimalist, highly performant On-Screen Display (OSD) and Heads-Up Display (HUD)
for Wayland compositors supporting the \fBwlr-layer-shell-unstable-v1\fR protocol
(such as River, Sway, Hyprland, and Niri).
.PP
Built with direct \fBlibwayland-client\fR and \fBCairo/Pango\fR rendering,
\fBwayhud\fR features a binary size under 60KB, consumes less than 8MB memory,
and starts in under 2ms. It is fully intangible: the input region is kept empty
so all pointer clicks pass straight through to windows beneath, and keyboard focus
is never intercepted.
.SH PIPELINE BEHAVIOR
.B wayhud
automatically detects its terminal environment via POSIX \fBisatty\fR(3):
.TP
.B Input Pipeline (\fI... | wayhud\fR)
When standard input is not a terminal, \fBwayhud\fR reads newline-delimited text
lines and projects each line as a clean, on-screen text frame. Long sentences wrap
automatically and are aligned according to the stylesheet. When EOF is reached,
the frame remains on screen for the duration specified by \fBtransition-duration\fR
before exiting cleanly.
.TP
.B Output Pipeline (\fIwayhud | ...\fR)
When standard output is piped, captured keystroke names are mirrored to stdout.
.TP
.B Standalone Keycaster
When executed directly in a terminal without pipe redirection, \fBwayhud\fR
reads kernel hardware input devices under \fI/dev/input/event*\fR. Rapid repetitive
keystrokes are automatically collapsed into a multiplier badge (e.g. \fBBackspace \(mu 4\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-style\fR \fI<file|css>\fR
Path to a GTK CSS stylesheet file, or an inline CSS string. If omitted,
\fBwayhud\fR searches for a configuration file according to the XDG Base Directory
Specification.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-name\fR \fI<name>\fR
Instance name used to match specific GTK CSS selectors (e.g. \fBwindow#\fR\fIname\fR
and \fBlabel#\fR\fIname\fR). Defaults to \fBkeys\fR.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help message and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information and exit.
.SH GTK CSS STYLING
Appearance, layout, and timing are configured using standard GTK CSS box model rules.
.SS Window Properties (\fBwindow\fR)
.TP
.B margin-top\fR, \fBmargin-bottom\fR, \fBmargin-left\fR, \fBmargin-right\fR, \fBmargin\fR
Margins in pixels. Margins also determine the Wayland anchor point:
setting \fBmargin-top\fR anchors to the top edge (centered horizontally unless left/right is given);
setting \fBmargin-bottom\fR and \fBmargin-left\fR anchors to the bottom-left corner.
.TP
.B padding-top\fR, \fBpadding-bottom\fR, \fBpadding-left\fR, \fBpadding-right\fR, \fBpadding\fR
Inner padding in pixels between the container edges and text.
.TP
.B width\fR, \fBmin-width\fR, \fBmax-width\fR
Dimensions in pixels. If \fBmin-width\fR is specified, the HUD will not shrink below this width
even when rendering short words, allowing stable centered banners.
.TP
.B background-color\fR, \fBbackground\fR
Background color in hex (\fI#rgb\fR, \fI#rrggbb\fR, \fI#rrggbbaa\fR),
\fIrgb(...)\fR, \fIrgba(...)\fR, or \fBtransparent\fR.
.TP
.B border-radius\fR
Radius of rounded corners in pixels.
.TP
.B border\fR, \fBborder-width\fR, \fBborder-color\fR
Border line thickness and color (e.g. \fI1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2)\fR).
.TP
.B transition-duration\fR
Display dwell and fade timeout in seconds or milliseconds (e.g. \fI1.2s\fR, \fI1200ms\fR).
Setting this to \fB0\fR enables permanent resident mode (the HUD stays visible until
new input arrives or the process terminates).
.SS Label Properties (\fBlabel\fR)
.TP
.B color
Foreground text color.
.TP
.B font-family\fR, \fBfont-size\fR, \fBfont-weight\fR, \fBfont\fR
Typography settings passed to Pango.
.TP
.B text-align
Horizontal text alignment: \fBcenter\fR, \fBleft\fR, or \fBright\fR.
.TP
.B text-shadow
Text outline or shadow following GTK CSS: \fIdx dy [blur] color\fR
(e.g. \fI0 0 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85)\fR) or \fBnone\fR.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
When no stylesheet is explicitly provided, \fBwayhud\fR resolves configuration files
in the following order:
.IP 1. 3
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wayhud/style.css\fR (defaults to \fI~/.config/wayhud/style.css\fR)
.IP 2. 3
Each directory in \fB$XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/wayhud/style.css\fR (defaults to \fI/etc/xdg/wayhud/style.css\fR)
.PP
Symbolic links are resolved via \fBrealpath\fR(3), fully supporting dotfile managers
such as GNU Stow, chezmoi, and yadm.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run standalone keyboard visualizer:
.RS 4
.B wayhud
.RE
.PP
Display an ephemeral notification from a script:
.RS 4
.B echo "Build Complete!" | wayhud
.RE
.PP
Display a live stream title centered below a top panel:
.RS 4
.B echo "🔴 Live Coding" | wayhud -n title
.RE
.PP
Pipe streaming speech-to-text transcripts with a centered caption bar:
.RS 4
.B whisper-stream | wayhud -n captions
.RE
.PP
Override style inline:
.RS 4
.B wayhud -s "window { margin-top: 30px; } label { color: #f38ba8; }"
.RE
